  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Brown-winged Striped Sweat Bee is a small, metallic green bee species belonging to the Agapostemon genus, commonly found in New England gardens and meadows. These solitary to semi-social bees are important pollinators that are attracted to human perspiration, hence their common name 'sweat bees.'",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Metallic Green Body",
      "description": "Distinctive iridescent green coloring on the head and thorax with a brown or dark abdomen",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown Wings",
      "description": "Notably darker, brownish wings that contrast with the bright green body coloration",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Typically 8-10mm in length, making them one of the smaller native bee species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Brown-winged Striped Sweat Bees thrive in open, sunny areas including meadows, gardens, woodland edges, and fields throughout New England where flowering plants are abundant.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Male and female Agapostemon splendens bees can look quite different, with females displaying the characteristic metallic green coloring while males often appear more golden or bronze, a phenomenon called sexual dimorphism.",
